        "problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}?$",
        "worked_solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(1,2),(1,0),$ and $(-1,-2).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,2)}=2(1)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2}=0, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,0)}=2(1)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}=2, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,-2)}=2(-1)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}(-2)^{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044888/assets/3dcb95f44f53f046c543d281038c0ac03fb14357cecdb7b96d5f41d33e9b0ed6.png)",
